  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 105 PM EST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nHigh pressure will edge east over the next several days. With \nthe slow transition, this will lead to modest temperatures\nincreases each day and possibly some lingering overnight/early\nmorning fog/stratus issues. Xover temps will be met tonight as\nwe head towards freezing once again and stagnant flow remains.\nConsidered adding patchy fog, but soundings suggest it may be\nmuch more shallow and isolated in nature for many areas. As a\nresult, left it out and deferred to evening shift to monitor \ntrends.\n\nA long wave trough, preparing to approach southern CA, will come\nonshore and eventually move NE into the Dakotas by 00Z Thu and \nthe U.P of MI by 00Z Fri. The overall upper flow will remain\nsouthwesterly across the region, with a push of warmer air into\nthe area. Highs will climb into the 60s for Thu-Sat. The area\nwill remain dry being removed from the best moisture and lift\nwell to the NW. \n\nWhile the aformentioned trough heads NE, another upper low \nsettles in further north, centering on Oregon by 6Z Fri. This \nfeature will eventually eject towards the region to bring what \nmay be the only chance for precip over the next 7 days. Timing \nlooks to be Saturday evening into Sunday morning, but overall \nrainfall amounts likely will be on the lower side. In the wake \nof this front, a return to more seasonable temps occurs Sunday \nand then well below normal (low to mid 40s) by Monday.",
